BIOGRAPHICAL SKETCH:
Christian Bird is a Senior Principal Researcher at Microsoft Research in Redmond and an ACM Distinguished Scientist. He analyzes the records that software projects leave behind: code changes, code reviews, build results, and bug reports, studied at scale to show how engineering teams really work and which practices measurably help. His current focus is AI-assisted development: building and studying the systems that let AI coding tools understand large codebases, and studying what those tools do to the people who use them. He has spent 16 years at Microsoft Research working alongside product teams on some of the largest codebases in existence, across code review, branching and release strategy, build systems, testing practice, and developer productivity. He holds a Ph.D. from UC Davis, advised by Prem Devanbu, and worked for five years as a software engineer at Motorola before graduate school. He co-edited the book The Art and Science of Analyzing Software Data (Morgan Kaufmann, 2015) and is an inventor on thirteen granted U.S. patents.

